1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,670
When I was one month old, I lost
about 90 to 95% of my hearing.

2
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:08,290
I’ve worn hearing aids ever since.

3
00:00:08,370 --> 00:00:13,910
They made accessibility a lot easier,
but they don’t solve every problem.

4
00:00:14,790 --> 00:00:20,950
One problem is that you can’t hear some
frequencies of sound, like consonants.

5
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:27,040
That makes it hard to tell these sounds
apart: s, f, th, sh, ch, k, p and h.

6
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:31,600
So when you’re a kid and
you can’t hear them, it’s

7
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:34,380
hard to learn how to pronounce words.

8
00:00:34,460 --> 00:00:37,990
For the longest time, I
said “chair” as “shair”,

9
00:00:38,070 --> 00:00:40,930
because I couldn’t hear the difference.

10
00:00:41,810 --> 00:00:43,980
It took a lot of speech therapy.

11
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:48,350
Trying to say what you literally
can’t hear is frustrating and

12
00:00:48,430 --> 00:00:52,510
time-consuming for the child,
the parent and the therapist.

13
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:59,490
It’s like doing maths with your dad at
the dinner table, but ten times worse.

14
00:00:59,570 --> 00:01:02,460
I wouldn’t wish it on my worst enemy.

15
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:06,790
I’m traumatised to this day,
and I’m not the only one.

16
00:01:07,670 --> 00:01:09,840
Here’s the problem we’re solving.

17
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:14,880
Research shows that children with
hearing loss are slowest to learn sounds

18
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:18,900
like S and SH, because hearing
aids can’t fully carry them.

19
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:24,140
They’re asked to say sounds they can’t
hear, with no way to check themselves.

20
00:01:25,020 --> 00:01:28,590
So we came up with a way
to visualise your voice.

21
00:01:28,670 --> 00:01:33,720
Soundbook is a gamified storytelling
app for deaf and hard of hearing

22
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:38,040
children, where they can see
what their voice sounds like.

23
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:42,630
The parent or therapist
picks the family of sounds

24
00:01:42,710 --> 00:01:45,210
they want their child to practise.

25
00:01:45,290 --> 00:01:48,470
Each family of sounds
is linked to a story.

26
00:01:49,350 --> 00:01:53,290
They pick a story and read
it aloud with their child.

27
00:01:53,370 --> 00:01:58,900
Throughout the story, the child is
asked to make letter sounds, like this.

28
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:00,770
Sssssss…

29
00:02:00,850 --> 00:02:03,860
I need practice, y’all.

30
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:07,900
I can’t do it at my grown age.

31
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:13,040
The slider shows the child how
soft or sharp their voice is,

32
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:16,220
which is very different
from quiet and loud.

33
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:21,790
They can see the difference between two
sounds, and get better with practice.

34
00:02:22,670 --> 00:02:25,900
The child’s voice moves the plot forward.

35
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:29,940
Here, Sid and his friend
need to pick where to go.

36
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:33,330
The child can make either sound to choose.

37
00:02:33,410 --> 00:02:36,390
I choose Shell Beach, so I say “Shhh”…

38
00:02:36,470 --> 00:02:37,600
and there I go.

39
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:43,650
The mic button shows the app is
listening, like on a video call.

40
00:02:43,730 --> 00:02:49,960
It fills green as the child makes a
sound, so they know they are being heard.

41
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:52,960
There is no red X anywhere.

42
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:57,920
If the sound is off, the coach
says which way to go: sharper,

43
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,910
like a tiny whistle, or
softer, like quiet waves.

44
00:03:01,990 --> 00:03:05,000
The dotted zone fills as they hold it.

45
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:09,790
A faint silhouette hints
at who is about to appear.

46
00:03:09,870 --> 00:03:13,850
Holding the sound brings
them into the story, so the

47
00:03:13,930 --> 00:03:16,970
reward is the story itself, not a score.

48
00:03:17,850 --> 00:03:22,640
The story comes in chapters,
and each one practises a sound.

49
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:27,020
A grown-up can filter to
just SSS or SHH for that day.

50
00:03:27,900 --> 00:03:34,370
Every sound earns a badge with the date,
so children can see their practice add up.

51
00:03:35,250 --> 00:03:38,620
Everything for adults
sits behind one button.

52
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:44,060
A parent or therapist can say the
sound once to set the target for this

53
00:03:44,140 --> 00:03:49,040
child, make the target easier,
skip a page, or play a demo voice.

54
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:55,610
For therapists, the sound lab shows where
the voice sits between SHH and SSS, and

55
00:03:55,690 --> 00:04:00,240
lets them calibrate both sounds
for one child and one microphone.

56
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:04,490
The progress report shows
sessions, how often each

57
00:04:04,570 --> 00:04:07,250
sound was held, and the trend over time.

58
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:12,970
Therapists can add notes and share it, so
home practice and therapy stay connected.

59
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:16,550
We designed for three people at once.

60
00:04:16,630 --> 00:04:19,560
The child gets a story with no settings.

61
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:22,790
The parent gets a simple
guide beside them.

62
00:04:22,870 --> 00:04:26,100
The therapist gets the
targets and the data.

63
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:33,350
We started with S and SH on purpose, because
the app can tell them apart reliably.

64
00:04:33,430 --> 00:04:37,920
Pairs like F and TH are hard
to detect, even for software,

65
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,140
so they come next instead of being faked.

66
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:47,040
Next, we would test with speech therapists
and families, tune the targets for

67
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:52,340
hearing-aid and cochlear-implant voices,
and add the next sounds, like CH and F.

68
00:04:53,220 --> 00:04:55,340
Speech therapy changed my life.

69
00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:59,300
Now my friends understand me
the first time, and I don’t

70
00:04:59,380 --> 00:05:02,420
think twice before
raising my hand in class.

71
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:03,830
But that took years.

72
00:05:03,910 --> 00:05:07,940
Soundbook doesn’t replace
speech therapy or human support.

73
00:05:08,020 --> 00:05:11,690
It makes the practice in
between easier and more fun,

74
00:05:11,770 --> 00:05:14,310
so the next kid can get there faster.

75
00:05:14,990 --> 00:05:18,240
AI accelerated our research and
our exploring of different design

76
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:21,660
directions, but we always used our
own judgement to see what works.

77
00:05:21,990 --> 00:05:24,910
Claude generated a lot of options
quickly, like particle stars,

78
00:05:24,990 --> 00:05:27,540
glowing orbs, glass sliders,
and dark and light themes.

79
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:30,160
We asked ourselves whether
each would actually work for

80
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:32,060
a five-year-old with a cochlear implant.

81
00:05:32,390 --> 00:05:35,760
The particle star was
beautiful, but children couldn’t

82
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:38,120
tell which way to change their sound.

83
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:41,700
Telling the two sounds apart
was the core problem, so we

84
00:05:41,780 --> 00:05:44,760
moved to a simple slider
between the two sounds.

85
00:05:45,090 --> 00:05:50,360
The glass knob looked really nice, but
a high-contrast ring works better to

86
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:54,560
show children where their sound
sits between the two sides.

87
00:05:54,890 --> 00:05:57,600
Dark mode looked polished,
but light mode felt more

88
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:00,060
familiar, more like a
storybook for children.

89
00:06:00,390 --> 00:06:05,620
We also added some of our own ideas,
like one-direction pages that flip, a

90
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:10,060
dotted target zone, and a
silhouette of who’s about to appear.

91
00:06:10,390 --> 00:06:12,130
We also redesigned the characters.

92
00:06:12,210 --> 00:06:14,910
AI gave us breadth, but our
judgement made the final

93
00:06:14,990 --> 00:06:17,260
call, and pushed the
bar for quality higher.

94
00:06:17,590 --> 00:06:20,710
Thank you so much for
hosting, and for watching.
